'''Mesopotàmia''' (from {{Langx|el|Μεσοποταμία}}, {{Literal translation|land between rivers}}; {{Langx|wap|Kuwarú|4=spreading water}}) is one of the four regions of the [[Estuaria|community of Estuaria]], within [[Balisca]]. It is the largest region by area within the community. It is bordered to north by the [[Salsipuedes Sea]], the northeast by [[Canyars]] and [[Trás-as-Montanyas]], to the south by the [[Bayara|Bayaran]] region [[Xaraba]], and [[Quiscosta]] to the west. The region's administrative seat and most populous city is [[Navassa]].

Various Indigenous groups, most prominently the [[Kurupiranga people|Guanarú people]], have inhabited this area for at least 8,000 years. In 1505, Volisanian explorer [[Aquil·les Balthazar Feixó]] became the first Illypnian to make landfall in Estuaria, calling the region ''la Mesopotàmia''' ({{W|IPA|[ˌmɛsəpəˈteɪmɪə]}}) for its complex river systems. Mesopotàmia subsequently became one of the earliest areas of permanent Illypno-Mesogean settlement in the [[Northern Triangle]]. The Volisanian colony of [[Navassa]], founded in 1535, is the fourth-oldest continuously inhabited, Mesogean-founded city in Balisca. Mesopotàmia was repeatedly contested between Volisanian and Xacano settlers, leading to the [[Reapers' War]] and the [[Expulsion of the Tarracos|expulsion of Cathars]] to [[Cerdanya]]. Mesopotàmia was occupied by [[Restaurasionistas]] early on in the [[Continuation War]], and remained under occupation until the decisive defeat of Royalist forces at the 1816 [[Battle of Navassa]]. Like the other predominately Volisanian-speaking territories, it rebelled against Bayaran authority during the [[Pastry War]], and during the [[Baliscan Civil War]], Mesopotàmia sided with the Confederalists, but was occupied by Bayaran forces, during which it experienced widespread destruction and looting. 

Urban environments in the region have a [[W:Polyethnicity|polyethnic]] and [[W:Multilingualism|multilingual]] heritage, being so strongly influenced by a mixture of 18th–century Volisan, [[W:Morisco|Morisco]], [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|Indigenous]], and Kaftian cultures that they are considered to be especially exceptional in Balisca. The importation of enslaved Kaftian people as slaves up to the end of the 18th century (typically from the same region of [[Central Kaftia|Central]] and [[Northern Kaftia]]), resulted in the concentration of Kaftian cultural influence.

